"text": " The most important penalty of his life, and he chipped it down the middle. June 20th, 1976. Belgrade. The European Championship final. Czechoslovakia against West Germany. 2-2 after extra time. For the first time, a European final goes to penalties. Seven kicks. Seven goals. Then, Juli Hones blasts West Germany's fourth over the bar. Antonin Panenka steps up. Score this, and Czechoslovakia are champions. In goal, World Cup winner Sepp Maier. Panenka runs in, Maier dives to his left, and Panenka chips it, softly, into the middle. He practised it after training, betting his club's goalkeeper a beer or a chocolate bar on every kick. Today, that penalty has a name. A Panenka. Would you dare to take it? Tell me in the comments.",
